Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server wrapping a REST API with stdio transport:

---

## Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a user by their ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The numeric user ID")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Search users by name. Returns matching results.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchUsers(
        [Description("Name or partial name to search for")] string query,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/users?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(query)}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool(Destructive = false, ReadOnly = true),
     Description("List all available API resources.")]
    public async Task<string> ListResources(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync("/resources", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

---

## `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: route logs to stderr so stdout stays clean for JSON-RPC
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for the API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The MCP SDK integrates directly with `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection`:

| Concept | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static `[McpServerToolType]` classes are instantiated per-call via DI. Services in the constructor (like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`) are resolved automatically. |
| **Method parameter injection** | On static classes, DI services can appear as method parameters. The SDK recognizes registered services and injects them — they're excluded from the tool's JSON schema. |
| **What's NOT injected** | Parameters with `[Description]` are user-facing inputs exposed in the schema. Only DI-registered types and special types (`CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<>`) are injected. |

**Key rule:** The SDK distinguishes tool inputs from injected services automatically. `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, `CancellationToken`, and any DI-registered service never appear in the LLM-facing schema — only `[Description]`-annotated parameters do.

To run: `dotnet build && dotnet run` — the server waits for JSON-RPC messages on stdin.
